  "why": "The statement asks for N(σ,T) ≪ T^(2(1−σ)+ε) on all of 1/2<σ<1. The classical ranges (Halász–Turán, Montgomery, Jutila, Bourgain σ≥25/32) are pre-snapshot cases; Ingham 1940 and Huxley are bounds on the exponent. Guth–Maynard (Annals 2026) lowers the sup exponent from 12/5 to 30/13, still above 2. I scored it bound, digested, since the sup of A(σ) is the quantity the hypothesis caps at 2. The OpenAI zero-free half-plane is a different, stronger statement (quasi-RH). It gives the density bound only for σ>7/8, which Bourgain already covered, so I scored it variant rather than a new case; it is AI-generated but does not move this problem. The Claude proportion-on-the-line results are a different quantity (variant), generated by AI. Alpöge–Furman counts as digested because Lamzouri reworked the argument in a published reproof. Pintz's 2025 lecture was the close call. Its abstract says 'prove the density hypothesis' but also invokes Halász's idea and names Bourgain as the record. I read it as a new proof of the known strip theorem, scored none and doubtful. Cheng and Bai are withdrawn.",
